   "reason": "No granular unit file at tests/unit/<layer>/test_<stem>.py. These 188 modules are not untested — they are untested BY A FILE THAT NAMES THEM. This tree's unit corpus is organised by BEHAVIOUR (test_recall.py, test_dream.py, test_checkup_tick.py) rather than by module, which is what the donor's M11 carve-out existed to forgive and what task 71 stopped forgiving: a file that references a module is not a file that goes red when that module alone breaks. Measured on the day of capture and re-measurable live with `python3 tests/_test_ownership.py --references`, the residual splits in two, and the split is the adjudicator's work order: 108 modules already have at least one same-layer unit file touching their anchor surface, so the remedy is to RENAME or SPLIT that file onto the module it actually exercises — no new coverage, a re-aim; the other 81 have nothing granular at all and their only coverage, where they have any, sits in integration/, e2e/ or gates/, which is exactly the constituency the retired carve-out was serving, so the remedy is to WRITE the unit file. By layer: ops 69, features 65, core 18, retrieval 18, knowledge 16, plus cli.py and strangler.py. It is declared rather than paid down here because task 71's contract is to land the RULE and the ratchet, and a task that also re-aimed 189 modules' worth of test corpus would make both unreviewable — the same decomposition tasks 67 and 68 made when they landed a gate and handed its residual on. Task 101 adjudicates: rename or split the covering file, write the missing unit file, or state why a module's behaviour genuinely cannot be exercised below the seam it sits on — and that last answer is a reason to MOVE the module, never to exempt it, because a row here may not be permanent.",
